      content:
          '**Brown-Sequard Syndrome -- Spinal Cord Hemisection**:\n\nThis syndrome results from damage to one half of the spinal cord, producing a characteristic pattern of deficits:\n\n**Ipsilateral Findings (Left -- same side as injury)**:\n1. **Motor weakness/paralysis (ipsilateral)**: Due to disruption of the **lateral corticospinal tract**, which has already decussated at the medullary pyramids. This patient\'s left-sided weakness (C5-S1) reflects damage to the left corticospinal tract at C5.\n2. **Loss of proprioception and vibration (ipsilateral)**: Due to disruption of the **dorsal columns** (fasciculus gracilis and cuneatus), which ascend ipsilaterally and do not decussate until the medulla. This patient has impaired position sense on the left.\n3. **Hyperreflexia and Babinski sign (ipsilateral)**: Upper motor neuron signs from corticospinal tract damage.\n4. **At-level pain/sensory changes**: Damage to the dorsal horn at the level of injury can produce ipsilateral dermatomal pain and reduced pin prick at C5-C8 (zone of injury).\n\n**Contralateral Findings (Right -- opposite side)**:\n1. **Loss of pain and temperature sensation (contralateral)**: Due to disruption of the **lateral spinothalamic tract**, which has already decussated at the anterior white commissure 1-2 levels below the dorsal root entry zone. This patient has absent pin prick on the right below C5.\n2. **Preserved light touch and proprioception (contralateral)**: The right dorsal columns are intact, and light touch is carried by multiple pathways (including the anterior spinothalamic tract on the contralateral side).\n\n**Prognosis -- Key Differentiator**:\nBrown-Sequard syndrome has the **best prognosis of all incomplete SCI patterns**:\n- **75-90%** of patients regain functional ambulation\n- The ipsilateral (weak) leg typically recovers enough strength for ambulation because some corticospinal fibers remain on the contralateral side (anterior corticospinal tract, ~10-15% of fibers, uncrossed)\n- Motor recovery begins proximally and progresses distally\n- Contralateral pain/temperature sensation often partially recovers via ipsilateral spinothalamic fibers\n- This is in stark contrast to anterior cord syndrome (worst motor prognosis) and central cord syndrome (variable)\n\n**ASIA Classification**: This patient is **AIS C** -- motor incomplete with sacral sparing (VAC present) and more than half of key muscles below the NLI grade <3. As she recovers, she may convert to AIS D (majority of muscles grade 3 or above).',
